tmkc:GC-0053 a skos:Concept,
        tmk:GroundOfRefusal,
        tmk:LegalConcept ;
    rdfs:comment "The Manual treats the grounds as a closed set fixed by the Act and Regulations. Whether a formality failure under paragraph 33(1)(a) is a 'ground for rejection' in the same sense as sections 39-44 is a real question the passage leaves open."@en-AU ;
    skos:altLabel "ground of rejection"@en-AU,
        "grounds for rejecting"@en-AU,
        "grounds for rejection"@en-AU ;
    skos:inScheme tmkc:scheme-s43 ;
    skos:prefLabel "ground for rejection"@en-AU ;
    tmk:authoredBy "claude-opus-5" ;
    tmk:authoredDate "2026-09-09"^^xsd:date ;
    tmk:authoringBasis "corpus_explicit" ;
    tmk:authoringReasoning "The Manual states in terms that the Act and Regulations contain all the grounds, and section 33(3) makes the Registrar's satisfaction that a ground exists the trigger for rejection. The concept is therefore the corpus's own, not a label imposed on it."@en-AU ;
    tmk:confidence 8e-01 ;
    tmk:definitionSource <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ref/TMM/Part20/5%231> ;
    tmk:expertShouldCheck "Whether a failure of the formality requirements under paragraph 33(1)(a) belongs inside this concept or beside it. The Manual's Part 10 treats formalities separately and section 33 lists them separately, which argues for beside."@en-AU ;
    tmk:goldRecord "GC-0053" ;
    tmk:legislativeBasis <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ref/TMA1995/s33>,
        <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ref/TMA1995/s33(1)(b)> ;
    tmk:notLabel "ground for revocation"@en-AU,
        "ground of opposition"@en-AU,
        "objection"@en-AU ;
    tmk:origin "authored" ;
    tmk:reviewStatus "unreviewed" ;
    tmk:sourcePassage <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ref/TMA1995/s33>,
        <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ref/TMM/Part20/5%231> ;
    tmk:typedBy tmka:GT-0053 .

tmkc:GC-0054 a skos:Concept,
        tmk:GroundOfRefusal,
        tmk:LegalConcept ;
    rdfs:comment "The Manual records that there are currently no regulations made for the purposes of section 18, so this ground is never raised in examination. The concept is real in law and inert in practice, and that gap is the most useful thing about the record."@en-AU ;
    skos:altLabel "prohibited signs"@en-AU,
        "sign that is not to be used as a trade mark"@en-AU ;
    skos:inScheme tmkc:scheme-s43 ;
    skos:prefLabel "prohibited sign"@en-AU ;
    tmk:authoredBy "claude-opus-5" ;
    tmk:authoredDate "2026-09-09"^^xsd:date ;
    tmk:authoringBasis "corpus_explicit" ;
    tmk:authoringReasoning "Section 39(1) names the class by reference to regulations under section 18, and the Manual states that no such regulations exist. Both halves are explicit, so the concept is recorded with its inertness attached rather than as a live ground."@en-AU ;
    tmk:confidence 8.5e-01 ;
    tmk:definitionSource <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ref/TMM/Part31/1%231> ;
    tmk:expertShouldCheck "Whether 'prohibited sign' is the right preferred label. The Manual's Part 31 title pairs it with 'prescribed', and the Act itself uses neither word for s 39(1)."@en-AU ;
    tmk:goldRecord "GC-0054" ;
    tmk:legislativeBasis <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ref/TMA1995/s18>,
        <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ref/TMA1995/s39(1)> ;
    tmk:notLabel "prescribed sign"@en-AU,
        "scandalous matter"@en-AU,
        "sign so nearly resembling"@en-AU ;
    tmk:origin "authored" ;
    tmk:reviewStatus "unreviewed" ;
    tmk:sourcePassage <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ref/TMA1995/s39>,
        <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ref/TMM/Part31/1%231> ;
    tmk:typedBy tmka:GT-0054 .

tmkc:GC-0055 a skos:Concept,
        tmk:GroundOfRefusal,
        tmk:LegalConcept ;
    rdfs:comment "Unlike s 39(1) this ground is live: the Manual records six types of prescribed sign in the regulations. Note the modality — subsection 39(2) says an application 'may' be rejected, where s 39(1) says 'must'."@en-AU ;
    skos:altLabel "prescribed signs"@en-AU,
        "sign prescribed for the purposes of subsection 39(2)"@en-AU ;
    skos:inScheme tmkc:scheme-s43 ;
    skos:prefLabel "prescribed sign"@en-AU ;
    tmk:authoredBy "claude-opus-5" ;
    tmk:authoredDate "2026-09-09"^^xsd:date ;
    tmk:authoringBasis "corpus_explicit" ;
    tmk:authoringReasoning "Both the Act and the Manual state the class in terms, and the Manual adds that six types are prescribed. The record keeps the 'may' visible because the difference between may and must across two subsections of one section is exactly the kind of thing a reader loses in a summary."@en-AU ;
    tmk:confidence 8.5e-01 ;
    tmk:definitionSource <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ref/TMM/Part31/2%231> ;
    tmk:expertShouldCheck "Whether the six prescribed types should each be a narrower concept. The Manual lists them at Part 31.4 and an examiner reasons about them individually."@en-AU ;
    tmk:goldRecord "GC-0055" ;
    tmk:legislativeBasis <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ref/TMA1995/s39(2)>,
        <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ref/TMA1995/s39(2)(a)> ;
    tmk:notLabel "prohibited sign"@en-AU,
        "scandalous matter"@en-AU ;
    tmk:origin "authored" ;
    tmk:reviewStatus "unreviewed" ;
    tmk:sourcePassage <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ref/TMA1995/s39>,
        <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ref/TMM/Part31/2%231> ;
    tmk:typedBy tmka:GT-0055 .

tmkc:GC-0056 a skos:Concept,
        tmk:LegalConcept,
        tmk:LegalTest ;
    rdfs:comment "A resemblance test that is not the section 44 one. The comparison is between the applicant's sign and a prescribed or prohibited sign, and the threshold is 'likely to be taken for it' rather than 'likely to deceive or cause confusion'."@en-AU ;
    skos:altLabel "so nearly resembling as to be likely to be taken for it"@en-AU ;
    skos:inScheme tmkc:scheme-s43 ;
    skos:prefLabel "so nearly resembling"@en-AU ;
    tmk:authoredBy "claude-opus-5" ;
    tmk:authoredDate "2026-09-09"^^xsd:date ;
    tmk:authoringBasis "corpus_explicit" ;
    tmk:authoringReasoning "The phrase is the Act's and the Manual quotes it as the operative test for subparagraph 39(2)(b)(i). It is held as a separate concept from deceptive similarity because the two use different words for different comparisons, and collapsing them would let a section 44 authority be cited into a section 39 question."@en-AU ;
    tmk:confidence 7.5e-01 ;
    tmk:definitionSource <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ref/TMM/Part31/2%231> ;
    tmk:expertShouldCheck "Whether practice in fact applies the section 10 case law to this test. If it does, the two concepts are more closely related than this record says."@en-AU ;
    tmk:goldRecord "GC-0056" ;
    tmk:legislativeBasis <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ref/TMA1995/s39(2)(b)> ;
    tmk:notLabel "deceptively similar"@en-AU,
        "substantially identical"@en-AU ;
    tmk:origin "authored" ;
    tmk:reviewStatus "unreviewed" ;
    tmk:sourcePassage <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ref/TMA1995/s39>,
        <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ref/TMM/Part31/2%231> ;
    tmk:typedBy tmka:GT-0056 .

tmkc:GC-0057 a skos:Concept,
        tmk:GroundOfRefusal,
        tmk:LegalConcept ;
    rdfs:comment "The shortest ground in the Act and the one most likely to be confused with a formality requirement. It bites on the mark itself, not on the paperwork."@en-AU ;
    skos:altLabel "graphical representation"@en-AU,
        "not able to be represented graphically"@en-AU ;
    skos:inScheme tmkc:scheme-s43 ;
    skos:prefLabel "cannot be represented graphically"@en-AU ;
    tmk:authoredBy "claude-opus-5" ;
    tmk:authoredDate "2026-09-09"^^xsd:date ;
    tmk:authoringBasis "corpus_explicit" ;
    tmk:authoringReasoning "Section 40 states the ground in one sentence. The concept is held because it is a distinct statutory ground, and it is worth distinguishing from the Part 10 formality about how a representation is filed — the Manual's Part 21 shows the two interacting for non-traditional signs."@en-AU ;
    tmk:confidence 8e-01 ;
    tmk:definitionSource <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ref/TMM/Part10/3%231> ;
    tmk:expertShouldCheck "The definition source. Part 10.3 is about representing the mark on the application; an expert may prefer a Part 21 passage that discusses s 40 directly."@en-AU ;
    tmk:goldRecord "GC-0057" ;
    tmk:legislativeBasis <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ref/TMA1995/s40> ;
    tmk:notLabel "non-traditional sign"@en-AU,
        "not capable of distinguishing"@en-AU ;
    tmk:origin "authored" ;
    tmk:reviewStatus "unreviewed" ;
    tmk:sourcePassage <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ref/TMA1995/s40> ;
    tmk:typedBy tmka:GT-0057 .

tmkc:GC-0058 a skos:Concept,
        tmk:LegalConcept,
        tmk:LegalTest ;
    rdfs:comment "The section 41 ground turns on this and nothing else; inherent adaptation and use are the two routes to it, not synonyms for it. The Manual's Part 22 is titled 'Section 41 - Capable of Distinguishing'."@en-AU ;
    skos:altLabel "capacity to distinguish"@en-AU,
        "not capable of distinguishing"@en-AU ;
    skos:inScheme tmkc:scheme-s43 ;
    skos:prefLabel "capable of distinguishing"@en-AU ;
    tmk:authoredBy "claude-opus-5" ;
    tmk:authoredDate "2026-09-09"^^xsd:date ;
    tmk:authoringBasis "corpus_explicit" ;
    tmk:authoringReasoning "Subsection 41(1) states the ground and subsection 41(2) makes 'taken not to be capable of distinguishing' depend on 41(3) or 41(4). The concept is therefore the top of a small structure the Manual works through in order, and it is held separately from inherent adaptation for that reason."@en-AU ;
    tmk:confidence 8.5e-01 ;
    tmk:definitionSource <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ref/TMM/Part22/1%231>,
        <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ref/TMM/Part22/3%231~1> ;
    tmk:expertShouldCheck "Whether 'distinctive' belongs in not_labels. It is the pre-1995 word and the Manual still uses it when discussing the 1955 Act, which may make it a historical alt_label rather than a near-miss."@en-AU ;
    tmk:goldRecord "GC-0058" ;
    tmk:legislativeBasis <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ref/TMA1995/s41>,
        <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ref/TMA1995/s41(1)>,
        <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ref/TMA1995/s41(2)> ;
    tmk:notLabel "deceptively similar"@en-AU,
        "distinctive"@en-AU,
        "inherently adapted to distinguish"@en-AU ;
    tmk:origin "authored" ;
    tmk:reviewStatus "unreviewed" ;
    tmk:sourcePassage <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ref/TMA1995/s41>,
        <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ref/TMM/Part22/3%231~1> ;
    tmk:typedBy tmka:GT-0058 .

tmkc:GC-0059 a skos:Concept,
        tmk:LegalConcept,
        tmk:LegalTest ;
    rdfs:comment "The Manual sets out a two-part process — ordinary signification, then the likelihood that other traders would legitimately wish to use the mark — and sorts every mark examined into one of three categories on the answer."@en-AU ;
    skos:altLabel "inherent adaptation"@en-AU,
        "inherent capacity to distinguish"@en-AU,
        "inherently adapted to distinguish"@en-AU ;
    skos:inScheme tmkc:scheme-s43 ;
    skos:prefLabel "inherent adaptation to distinguish"@en-AU ;
    tmk:authoredBy "claude-opus-5" ;
    tmk:authoredDate "2026-09-09"^^xsd:date ;
    tmk:authoringBasis "corpus_explicit" ;
    tmk:authoringReasoning "The Manual states the question and its two-part method in terms. The concept is separate from capability because the Act treats inherent adaptation as one input to capability and use as the other, and the three-category sort in Part 22.3 only makes sense against inherent adaptation alone."@en-AU ;
    tmk:confidence 8.5e-01 ;
    tmk:definitionSource <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ref/TMM/Part22/3%231~1> ;
    tmk:expertShouldCheck "Whether 'inherent capacity to distinguish' really is a synonym. The Manual uses 'capacity' and 'adaptation' in adjacent sentences and an expert may hold them apart."@en-AU ;
    tmk:goldRecord "GC-0059" ;
    tmk:legislativeBasis <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ref/TMA1995/s41(3)(a)>,
        <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ref/TMA1995/s41(4)(a)> ;
    tmk:notLabel "acquired distinctiveness"@en-AU,
        "capable of distinguishing"@en-AU,
        "evidence of use"@en-AU ;
    tmk:origin "authored" ;
    tmk:reviewStatus "unreviewed" ;
    tmk:sourcePassage <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ref/TMA1995/s41>,
        <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ref/TMM/Part22/3%231~1> ;
    tmk:typedBy tmka:GT-0059 .

tmkc:GC-0060 a skos:Concept,
        tmk:LegalConcept,
        tmk:LegalTest ;
    rdfs:comment "The first half of the Manual's two-part inherent-adaptation process. It is assessed 'in the light of the applied for goods and/or services', so it is not the word's meaning in the abstract."@en-AU ;
    skos:altLabel "ordinary signification of the trade mark"@en-AU ;
    skos:inScheme tmkc:scheme-s43 ;
    skos:prefLabel "ordinary signification"@en-AU ;
    tmk:authoredBy "claude-opus-5" ;
    tmk:authoredDate "2026-09-09"^^xsd:date ;
    tmk:authoringBasis "corpus_explicit" ;
    tmk:authoringReasoning "The Manual names it as the first step of a two-part process and ties it to the goods and services applied for. It is held apart from 'connotation' — a section 43 concept this repository already holds — because the two ask different questions of the same mark and share no statutory text."@en-AU ;
    tmk:confidence 8e-01 ;
    tmk:definitionSource <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ref/TMM/Part22/3%231~1> ;
    tmk:expertShouldCheck "The relationship to connotation. An expert may see them as two names for one underlying idea applied under different sections, which would make this a 'related' rather than a distinct concept."@en-AU ;
    tmk:goldRecord "GC-0060" ;
    tmk:legislativeBasis <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ref/TMA1995/s41(3)>,
        <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ref/TMA1995/s41(4)> ;
    tmk:notLabel "connotation"@en-AU,
        "denotation"@en-AU,
        "dictionary meaning"@en-AU ;
    tmk:origin "authored" ;
    tmk:reviewStatus "unreviewed" ;
    tmk:sourcePassage <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ref/TMM/Part22/3%231~1> ;
    tmk:typedBy tmka:GT-0060 .

tmkc:GC-0061 a skos:Concept,
        tmk:LegalConcept,
        tmk:LegalTest ;
    rdfs:comment "The second half of the two-part process. The label is this repository's, not the Manual's — the Manual states the idea in a clause rather than naming it — and that is the most likely thing to be wrong about this record."@en-AU ;
    skos:altLabel "other traders might legitimately wish to use"@en-AU,
        "the need of other traders"@en-AU ;
    skos:inScheme tmkc:scheme-s43 ;
    skos:prefLabel "other traders' legitimate desire to use"@en-AU ;
    tmk:authoredBy "claude-opus-5" ;
    tmk:authoredDate "2026-09-09"^^xsd:date ;
    tmk:authoringBasis "corpus_explicit" ;
    tmk:authoringReasoning "The corpus states the idea in terms but gives it no name, so the record is corpus_explicit as to the content and an invention as to the label. It is held because an examiner reasons with it constantly and a vocabulary without it cannot represent the second half of the Manual's own method."@en-AU ;
    tmk:confidence 6e-01 ;
    tmk:definitionSource <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ref/TMM/Part22/3%231~1> ;
    tmk:expertShouldCheck "The preferred label, which no passage supports. The case law phrase is closer to 'the legitimate desire of other traders', and an expert should replace this label rather than accept it."@en-AU ;
    tmk:goldRecord "GC-0061" ;
    tmk:legislativeBasis <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ref/TMA1995/s41(3)>,
        <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ref/TMA1995/s41(4)> ;
    tmk:notLabel "honest concurrent use"@en-AU,
        "ordinary signification"@en-AU ;
    tmk:origin "authored" ;
    tmk:reviewStatus "unreviewed" ;
    tmk:sourcePassage <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ref/TMM/Part22/3%231~1> ;
    tmk:typedBy tmka:GT-0061 .

tmkc:GC-0062 a skos:Concept,
        tmk:GroundOfRefusal,
        tmk:LegalConcept ;
    rdfs:comment "One of the two limbs of section 42; the Manual's Part 30 pairs it with use contrary to law but the Act keeps them as separate paragraphs, and only this one is about the mark's content rather than the consequences of using it."@en-AU ;
    skos:altLabel "scandalous sign"@en-AU,
        "scandalous signs"@en-AU ;
    skos:inScheme tmkc:scheme-s43 ;
    skos:prefLabel "scandalous matter"@en-AU ;
    tmk:authoredBy "claude-opus-5" ;
    tmk:authoredDate "2026-09-09"^^xsd:date ;
    tmk:authoringBasis "corpus_explicit" ;
    tmk:authoringReasoning "Both the Act and the Manual state the ground in one sentence. Held apart from 'use contrary to law' because paragraph 42(a) looks at what the mark contains and paragraph 42(b) at what using it would do — different evidence answers each."@en-AU ;
    tmk:confidence 8.5e-01 ;
    tmk:definitionSource <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ref/TMM/Part30/1%231>,
        <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ref/TMM/Part30/2%231> ;
    tmk:expertShouldCheck "Whether the preferred label should be the Act's 'scandalous matter' or the Manual's heading 'scandalous signs'. The Act's phrase is narrower — matter within a sign — and this record follows the Act."@en-AU ;
    tmk:goldRecord "GC-0062" ;
    tmk:legislativeBasis <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ref/TMA1995/s42(a)> ;
    tmk:notLabel "prescribed sign"@en-AU,
        "prohibited sign"@en-AU,
        "use contrary to law"@en-AU ;
    tmk:origin "authored" ;
    tmk:reviewStatus "unreviewed" ;
    tmk:sourcePassage <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ref/TMA1995/s42>,
        <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ref/TMM/Part30/2%231> ;
    tmk:typedBy tmka:GT-0062 .

tmkc:GC-0063 a skos:Concept,
        tmk:GroundOfRefusal,
        tmk:LegalConcept ;
    rdfs:comment "The second limb of section 42. The Manual's Annex A1 lists the other legislation that may trigger it, which is what makes this ground unusual: its content lives outside the Trade Marks Act entirely."@en-AU ;
    skos:altLabel "contrary to law"@en-AU,
        "use would be contrary to law"@en-AU ;
    skos:inScheme tmkc:scheme-s43 ;
    skos:prefLabel "use contrary to law"@en-AU ;
    tmk:authoredBy "claude-opus-5" ;
    tmk:authoredDate "2026-09-09"^^xsd:date ;
    tmk:authoringBasis "corpus_explicit" ;
    tmk:authoringReasoning "Stated in terms by both the Act and the Manual. Held separately from scandalous matter for the reason given on that record."@en-AU ;
    tmk:confidence 8.5e-01 ;
    tmk:definitionSource <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ref/TMM/Part30/1%231>,
        <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ref/TMM/Part30/3%231> ;
    tmk:expertShouldCheck "Whether the ground reaches use that would be contrary to law only in some circumstances. The Manual's annexes suggest practice draws that line, and this record does not carry it."@en-AU ;
    tmk:goldRecord "GC-0063" ;
    tmk:legislativeBasis <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ref/TMA1995/s42(b)> ;
    tmk:notLabel "prohibited sign"@en-AU,
        "scandalous matter"@en-AU ;
    tmk:origin "authored" ;
    tmk:reviewStatus "unreviewed" ;
    tmk:sourcePassage <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ref/TMA1995/s42>,
        <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ref/TMM/Part30/3%231> ;
    tmk:typedBy tmka:GT-0063 .

tmkc:GC-0064 a skos:Concept,
        tmk:GroundOfRefusal,
        tmk:LegalConcept ;
    rdfs:comment "The Manual's Part 26 title is 'Section 44 and Regulation 4.15A - Conflict with Other Signs'. Note that the Manual states the purpose of the section as preventing confusion, which is the same language section 43 uses for a different ground — the two must not be collapsed."@en-AU ;
    skos:altLabel "citation"@en-AU,
        "conflicting trade mark"@en-AU,
        "section 44 ground"@en-AU ;
    skos:inScheme tmkc:scheme-s43 ;
    skos:prefLabel "conflict with other signs"@en-AU ;
    tmk:authoredBy "claude-opus-5" ;
    tmk:authoredDate "2026-09-09"^^xsd:date ;
    tmk:authoringBasis "corpus_explicit" ;
    tmk:authoringReasoning "Held as the ground itself, with the comparison tests beneath it as separate concepts. The alt_label 'citation' is included because that is what examiners call the earlier mark in practice and a vocabulary that could not match it would miss most of Part 26 and all of Part 27."@en-AU ;
    tmk:confidence 7.5e-01 ;
    tmk:definitionSource <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ref/TMM/Part26/1%231> ;
    tmk:expertShouldCheck "Whether 'citation' is really a synonym for the ground or the name of the cited mark. If the second, it is a separate concept and this alt_label is wrong."@en-AU ;
    tmk:goldRecord "GC-0064" ;
    tmk:legislativeBasis <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ref/TMA1995/s44>,
        <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ref/TMA1995/s44(1)>,
        <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ref/TMA1995/s44(2)>,
        <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ref/TMR1995/r4.15A> ;
    tmk:notLabel "likely to deceive or cause confusion"@en-AU,
        "prescribed sign"@en-AU ;
    tmk:origin "authored" ;
    tmk:reviewStatus "unreviewed" ;
    tmk:sourcePassage <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ref/TMA1995/s44>,
        <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ref/TMM/Part26/1%231> ;
    tmk:typedBy tmka:GT-0064 .

tmkc:GC-0065 a skos:Concept,
        tmk:LegalConcept,
        tmk:LegalTest ;
    rdfs:comment "One of the two comparisons section 44 turns on, and the only one the Act does not define. The Manual gives it no definition either — it states that the ground depends on the two comparisons and then works through the case law."@en-AU ;
    skos:altLabel "substantially identical with"@en-AU ;
    skos:inScheme tmkc:scheme-s43 ;
    skos:prefLabel "substantially identical"@en-AU ;
    tmk:authoredBy "claude-opus-5" ;
    tmk:authoredDate "2026-09-09"^^xsd:date ;
    tmk:authoringBasis "corpus_explicit" ;
    tmk:authoringReasoning "The corpus names the test in terms and pairs it with deceptive similarity without defining it. The record says so rather than supplying a definition from general knowledge, which is what ADR-0079 guard 2 requires."@en-AU ;
    tmk:confidence 8e-01 ;
    tmk:definitionSource <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ref/TMM/Part26/5%231> ;
    tmk:expertShouldCheck "That this record deliberately carries no definition. If the Manual defines the test somewhere this pass did not reach, the definition_sources are wrong rather than incomplete."@en-AU ;
    tmk:goldRecord "GC-0065" ;
    tmk:legislativeBasis <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ref/TMA1995/s44(1)(a)>,
        <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ref/TMA1995/s44(2)(a)> ;
    tmk:notLabel "deceptively similar"@en-AU,
        "identical"@en-AU,
        "so nearly resembling"@en-AU ;
    tmk:origin "authored" ;
    tmk:reviewStatus "unreviewed" ;
    tmk:sourcePassage <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ref/TMA1995/s44>,
        <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ref/TMM/Part26/5%231> ;
    tmk:typedBy tmka:GT-0065 .

tmkc:GC-0066 a skos:Concept,
        tmk:Exception,
        tmk:LegalConcept ;
    rdfs:comment "The Manual sets out five criteria and makes honesty a threshold requirement without which paragraph 44(3)(a) cannot be satisfied. Note the modality: if satisfied, the Registrar *may* accept — this is a discretion, not an entitlement."@en-AU ;
    skos:altLabel "concurrent use"@en-AU,
        "honest concurrent use of the 2 trade marks"@en-AU ;
    skos:inScheme tmkc:scheme-s43 ;
    skos:prefLabel "honest concurrent use"@en-AU ;
    tmk:authoredBy "claude-opus-5" ;
    tmk:authoredDate "2026-09-09"^^xsd:date ;
    tmk:authoringBasis "corpus_explicit" ;
    tmk:authoringReasoning "The Manual states what the doctrine is for, how it is established and that honesty is a threshold. Held as an exception rather than as a ground because it takes a case out of a ground that would otherwise apply."@en-AU ;
    tmk:confidence 8.5e-01 ;
    tmk:definitionSource <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ref/TMM/Part28/2%231>,
        <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ref/TMM/Part28/3%231> ;
    tmk:expertShouldCheck "Whether the five criteria should each be a narrower concept. The Manual lists them at 28.2 and says no single criterion is determinative, which is the sort of structure a knowledge graph is good at and a flat concept is not."@en-AU ;
    tmk:goldRecord "GC-0066" ;
    tmk:legislativeBasis <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ref/TMA1995/s44(3)(a)> ;
    tmk:notLabel "evidence of use"@en-AU,
        "other circumstances"@en-AU,
        "prior use"@en-AU ;
    tmk:origin "authored" ;
    tmk:reviewStatus "unreviewed" ;
    tmk:sourcePassage <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ref/TMM/Part28/2%231>,
        <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ref/TMM/Part28/3%231> ;
    tmk:typedBy tmka:GT-0066 .

tmkc:GC-0067 a skos:Concept,
        tmk:Exception,
        tmk:LegalConcept ;
    rdfs:comment "Distinguished from honest concurrent use by its modality as much as its content: where s 44(3) says the Registrar *may* accept, s 44(4) says the Registrar *may not reject*. The Manual sets out the elements an applicant must satisfy."@en-AU ;
    skos:altLabel "continuous prior use"@en-AU,
        "prior continuous use"@en-AU ;
    skos:inScheme tmkc:scheme-s43 ;
    skos:prefLabel "prior use"@en-AU ;
    tmk:authoredBy "claude-opus-5" ;
    tmk:authoredDate "2026-09-09"^^xsd:date ;
    tmk:authoringBasis "corpus_explicit" ;
    tmk:authoringReasoning "The Manual states the provision, the period and the elements. The record keeps the 'may not reject' wording in evidence because that modality is the difference between this and honest concurrent use and it is easily lost in paraphrase."@en-AU ;
    tmk:confidence 8.5e-01 ;
    tmk:definitionSource <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ref/TMM/Part28/6%231> ;
    tmk:expertShouldCheck "Whether continuity of use is a separate concept. The Manual makes 'continuous' do a lot of work in the elements list and an expert may want it held apart."@en-AU ;
    tmk:goldRecord "GC-0067" ;
    tmk:legislativeBasis <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ref/TMA1995/s44(4)> ;
    tmk:notLabel "first use"@en-AU,
        "honest concurrent use"@en-AU,
        "other circumstances"@en-AU ;
    tmk:origin "authored" ;
    tmk:reviewStatus "unreviewed" ;
    tmk:sourcePassage <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ref/TMA1995/s44>,
        <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ref/TMM/Part28/6%231> ;
    tmk:typedBy tmka:GT-0067 .

tmkc:GC-0068 a skos:Concept,
        tmk:Exception,
        tmk:LegalConcept ;
    rdfs:comment "The Manual grounds this in the commercial realities that make acceptance proper notwithstanding a real risk of deception or confusion. It is the most open-textured of the three ways round section 44."@en-AU ;
    skos:altLabel "other circumstances making it proper to accept"@en-AU ;
    skos:inScheme tmkc:scheme-s43 ;
    skos:prefLabel "other circumstances"@en-AU ;
    tmk:authoredBy "claude-opus-5" ;
    tmk:authoredDate "2026-09-09"^^xsd:date ;
    tmk:authoringBasis "corpus_explicit" ;
    tmk:authoringReasoning "The Manual states what the paragraph turns on in one sentence. Held separately from honest concurrent use because paragraph 44(3)(b) is an alternative to 44(3)(a), not a gloss on it."@en-AU ;
    tmk:confidence 8e-01 ;
    tmk:definitionSource <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ref/TMM/Part28/4%231> ;
    tmk:expertShouldCheck "Whether 'notwithstanding a real risk of deception or confusion' should appear in the concept at all. It is the Manual's gloss on Bali No. 2 rather than the statutory test, and it is doing a lot of work in this record."@en-AU ;
    tmk:goldRecord "GC-0068" ;
    tmk:legislativeBasis <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ref/TMA1995/s44(3)(b)> ;
    tmk:notLabel "conditions and limitations"@en-AU,
        "honest concurrent use"@en-AU,
        "prior use"@en-AU ;
    tmk:origin "authored" ;
    tmk:reviewStatus "unreviewed" ;
    tmk:sourcePassage <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ref/TMM/Part28/4%231> ;
    tmk:typedBy tmka:GT-0068 .

tmkc:GC-0069 a skos:Concept,
        tmk:LegalConcept ;
    rdfs:comment "The Act defines 'limitations' in section 6 and uses 'conditions or limitations' as a pair in sections 33(2) and 44(3). The Manual treats them as the price of acceptance under subsection 44(3)."@en-AU ;
    skos:altLabel "conditions and limitations"@en-AU,
        "limitations"@en-AU ;
    skos:inScheme tmkc:scheme-s43 ;
    skos:prefLabel "conditions or limitations"@en-AU ;
    tmk:authoredBy "claude-opus-5" ;
    tmk:authoredDate "2026-09-09"^^xsd:date ;
    tmk:authoringBasis "corpus_explicit" ;
    tmk:authoringReasoning "The Act states the power and defines one half of the pair. The concept is held as the pair because that is how both the Act and the Manual use it, and holding 'limitations' alone would leave 'conditions' unrepresented."@en-AU ;
    tmk:confidence 7.5e-01 ;
    tmk:definitionSource <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ref/TMM/Part28/5%231> ;
    tmk:expertShouldCheck "Whether conditions and limitations are one concept or two. The Act defines only limitations, which is evidence for two."@en-AU ;
    tmk:goldRecord "GC-0069" ;
    tmk:legislativeBasis <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ref/TMA1995/s33(2)>,
        <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ref/TMA1995/s44(3)>,
        <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ref/TMA1995/s6/limitations> ;
    tmk:notLabel "disclaimer"@en-AU,
        "endorsement"@en-AU,
        "other circumstances"@en-AU ;
    tmk:origin "authored" ;
    tmk:reviewStatus "unreviewed" ;
    tmk:sourcePassage <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ref/TMA1995/s44>,
        <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ref/TMA1995/s6/limitations> ;
    tmk:typedBy tmka:GT-0069 .

tmkc:GC-0070 a skos:Concept,
        tmk:LegalConcept,
        tmk:LegalTest ;
    rdfs:comment "The Manual is explicit that classification is not the determining factor, which is the single most useful thing in this record: the class number and the similarity question are different things and are routinely confused."@en-AU ;
    skos:altLabel "goods of the same description"@en-AU,
        "similar goods"@en-AU,
        "similar services"@en-AU ;
    skos:inScheme tmkc:scheme-s43 ;
    skos:prefLabel "similarity of goods and services"@en-AU ;
    tmk:authoredBy "claude-opus-5" ;
    tmk:authoredDate "2026-09-09"^^xsd:date ;
    tmk:authoringBasis "corpus_explicit" ;
    tmk:authoringReasoning "Section 14 defines the term and the Manual states the practice point that classification does not settle it. Both are explicit."@en-AU ;
    tmk:confidence 8.5e-01 ;
    tmk:definitionSource <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ref/TMM/Part26/4%231> ;
    tmk:expertShouldCheck "Whether 'goods of the same description' is a synonym or a narrower concept. Section 14(1)(b) makes it one of two ways goods can be similar, which argues narrower."@en-AU ;
    tmk:goldRecord "GC-0070" ;
    tmk:legislativeBasis <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ref/TMA1995/s14>,
        <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ref/TMA1995/s14(1)>,
        <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ref/TMA1995/s14(2)> ;
    tmk:notLabel "classification of goods and services"@en-AU,
        "closely related goods and services"@en-AU ;
    tmk:origin "authored" ;
    tmk:reviewStatus "unreviewed" ;
    tmk:sourcePassage <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ref/TMA1995/s14>,
        <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ref/TMM/Part26/4%231> ;
    tmk:typedBy tmka:GT-0070 .

tmkc:GC-0071 a skos:Concept,
        tmk:LegalConcept,
        tmk:LegalTest ;
    rdfs:comment "Section 44 pairs 'similar goods' with 'closely related services' and vice versa. Unlike similarity, the Act does not define closeness of relation anywhere."@en-AU ;
    skos:altLabel "closely related goods"@en-AU,
        "closely related services"@en-AU ;
    skos:inScheme tmkc:scheme-s43 ;
    skos:prefLabel "closely related goods and services"@en-AU ;
    tmk:authoredBy "claude-opus-5" ;
    tmk:authoredDate "2026-09-09"^^xsd:date ;
    tmk:authoringBasis "corpus_explicit" ;
    tmk:authoringReasoning "The Act uses the phrase as an operative term and defines it nowhere, so the record holds the concept and says the corpus does not define it. Held apart from similarity because the two appear in the same paragraph doing different work."@en-AU ;
    tmk:confidence 7e-01 ;
    tmk:definitionSource <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ref/TMM/Part26/4%231> ;
    tmk:expertShouldCheck "Whether the Manual defines this somewhere in Part 26 that this pass did not reach. The record's definition_sources point at the similarity discussion, which is adjacent rather than on point."@en-AU ;
    tmk:goldRecord "GC-0071" ;
    tmk:legislativeBasis <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ref/TMA1995/s44(1)(a)>,
        <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ref/TMA1995/s44(2)(a)> ;
    tmk:notLabel "similar goods"@en-AU,
        "similar services"@en-AU ;
    tmk:origin "authored" ;
    tmk:reviewStatus "unreviewed" ;
    tmk:sourcePassage <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ref/TMA1995/s44> ;
    tmk:typedBy tmka:GT-0071 .

tmkc:GC-0072 a skos:Concept,
        tmk:LegalConcept ;
    rdfs:comment "The Manual makes the point that matters most here: the definition of trade mark is closed and exhaustive, while the definition of sign is open and inclusive. A sign is a trade mark only when used or intended to be used to distinguish in the course of trade."@en-AU ;
    skos:altLabel "mark"@en-AU ;
    skos:inScheme tmkc:scheme-s43 ;
    skos:prefLabel "trade mark"@en-AU ;
    tmk:authoredBy "claude-opus-5" ;
    tmk:authoredDate "2026-09-09"^^xsd:date ;
    tmk:authoringBasis "corpus_explicit" ;
    tmk:authoringReasoning "Section 17 defines it in terms and the Manual explains the character of the definition. Held apart from 'sign' because the Manual says in one sentence why they are not the same kind of definition."@en-AU ;
    tmk:confidence 9e-01 ;
    tmk:definitionSource <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ref/TMM/Part20/1%231>,
        <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ref/TMM/Part20/3%231> ;
    tmk:expertShouldCheck "Whether 'mark' is really an alt_label. The Manual uses it as the 1955 Act's word and the 1995 Act deliberately replaced it, which may make it historical rather than current."@en-AU ;
    tmk:goldRecord "GC-0072" ;
    tmk:legislativeBasis <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ref/TMA1995/s17>,
        <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ref/TMA1995/s6/trade-mark> ;
    tmk:notLabel "business name"@en-AU,
        "registered trade mark"@en-AU,
        "sign"@en-AU,
        "trade name"@en-AU ;
    tmk:origin "authored" ;
    tmk:reviewStatus "unreviewed" ;
    tmk:sourcePassage <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ref/TMA1995/s17>,
        <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ref/TMM/Part20/3%231> ;
    tmk:typedBy tmka:GT-0072 .

tmkc:GC-0073 a skos:Concept,
        tmk:LegalConcept ;
    rdfs:comment "Section 6's list is inclusive, so the enumerated items — letter, word, name, signature, numeral, device, brand, heading, label, ticket, aspect of packaging, shape, colour, sound, scent — are examples rather than a closed set. That is the whole basis of the Manual's Part 21 on non-traditional signs."@en-AU ;
    skos:inScheme tmkc:scheme-s43 ;
    skos:prefLabel "sign"@en-AU ;
    tmk:authoredBy "claude-opus-5" ;
    tmk:authoredDate "2026-09-09"^^xsd:date ;
    tmk:authoringBasis "corpus_explicit" ;
    tmk:authoringReasoning "Defined by the Act and characterised by the Manual, both in terms."@en-AU ;
    tmk:confidence 9e-01 ;
    tmk:definitionSource <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ref/TMM/Part20/3%231>,
        <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ref/TMM/Part21/1%231> ;
    tmk:expertShouldCheck "Whether the enumerated items should be narrower concepts now. Part 21 develops shape, colour, sound, scent and moving images and leaves the rest as a list."@en-AU ;
    tmk:goldRecord "GC-0073" ;
    tmk:legislativeBasis <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ref/TMA1995/s6/sign> ;
    tmk:notLabel "device"@en-AU,
        "representation of the trade mark"@en-AU,
        "trade mark"@en-AU ;
    tmk:origin "authored" ;
    tmk:reviewStatus "unreviewed" ;
    tmk:sourcePassage <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ref/TMA1995/s6/sign>,
        <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ref/TMM/Part20/3%231> ;
    tmk:typedBy tmka:GT-0073 .

tmkc:GC-0074 a skos:Concept,
        tmk:LegalConcept ;
    rdfs:comment "Not a statutory category. The Manual groups shapes, colours, sounds, scents, moving images, holograms and gestures under this heading because the 1995 Act broadened the definition of a trade mark to reach them."@en-AU ;
    skos:altLabel "non-traditional signs"@en-AU,
        "non-traditional trade mark"@en-AU ;
    skos:inScheme tmkc:scheme-s43 ;
    skos:prefLabel "non-traditional sign"@en-AU ;
    tmk:authoredBy "claude-opus-5" ;
    tmk:authoredDate "2026-09-09"^^xsd:date ;
    tmk:authoringBasis "corpus_inferred" ;
    tmk:authoringReasoning "The Manual devotes a Part to the category and names it in the Part title, but never defines it — so the record is corpus_inferred and says so. It is held because the grouping drives practice: these are the signs where section 40 and the representation requirements bite hardest."@en-AU ;
    tmk:confidence 7e-01 ;
    tmk:definitionSource <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ref/TMM/Part21/1%231> ;
    tmk:expertShouldCheck "Which signs are inside the category. This record takes the Manual's Part 21 contents as the extension, and an expert may draw it differently."@en-AU ;
    tmk:goldRecord "GC-0074" ;
    tmk:legislativeBasis <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ref/TMA1995/s40>,
        <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ref/TMA1995/s6/sign> ;
    tmk:notLabel "composite trade mark"@en-AU,
        "device"@en-AU,
        "sign"@en-AU ;
    tmk:origin "authored" ;
    tmk:reviewStatus "unreviewed" ;
    tmk:sourcePassage <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ref/TMM/Part21/1%231> ;
    tmk:typedBy tmka:GT-0074 .

tmkc:GC-0075 a skos:Concept,
        tmk:LegalConcept ;
    rdfs:comment "Registrable because section 6 names 'shape' in the definition of sign. The Manual records that this reversed the pre-1995 position, in which a mark had to be something separate from the goods."@en-AU ;
    skos:altLabel "shape (three-dimensional) trade marks"@en-AU,
        "three-dimensional trade mark"@en-AU ;
    skos:inScheme tmkc:scheme-s43 ;
